            "url": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrapoza/2019/05/09/heres-how-much-trumps-tariffs-cost-chinas-gdp/",
            "title": "Here's How Much Trump's Tariffs Will Cost China's GDP",
            "snippets": [
              "# Here's How Much Trump's Tariffs Will Cost China's GDP\nThe higher costs resulting from the tariffs have yet to inflict much overall damage to a still-robust American economy, which is less reliant on international trade than most other countries. Spurred by lower taxes, the economy grew at an impressive 3.2% percent in the first quarter, better than most emerging markets.",
              "If Trump went ahead and tariffed a combined $500 billion of Chinese imports, it would be the equivalent of roughly 4% of China's $13.5 trillion GDP. If the U.S. had tariffs on half the value of Chinese imports, as it now stands, then it is the equivalent of roughly 2% of GDP",
              "\"We’re are not completely bearish on China,\" says Vladimir Signorelli, founder of Bretton Woods Research, an investment research firm using supply-side economics. \"Stimulus moves have so far blunted the deleveraging that was primarily behind China’s slowdown last year. If Xi’s intent is to wait out the Trump Administration, such tariff increases may be acceptable as the cost of doing business with the U.S. for the time being.\"",
              "Tariffs are here to stay. Over the last year, China has been dealing with tariffs, using stimulus measures to hit its 6%+ growth target",
              "Aidan Yao, senior China economist for AXA Investment Managers says a full-blown trade war with everything tariffed will trim 1.5% off China's GDP. \"The authorities, in that case, will likely step up policy supports. But with limited scope to stimulate, Beijing will have to make some tough choices, such as easing housing-market and shadow-banking policies,\" Yao says",
              "The latter could stabilize growth, but they can also risk worsening China’s structural imbalances, giving rise to policy mistakes further down the road. This is something Xi has prided himself on avoiding"
            ]
